  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Potential Browser Crashes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on potential issues. I\'ve realized `AIInsightsCard` is just a card, so I\'m investigating `aiService.ts` and its import of `@google/genai`. I\'m trying to determine whether importing `GoogleGenAI` from `@google/genai` causes the app to crash in the browser. My current effort is focused on that critical dependency.\n\n\n**Confirming Root Cause**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the \"process is not defined\" error. That import of `@google/genai` is the likely culprit, because it references `process.env`. My thinking now is that Vite\'s `define` feature *should* replace `process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY`, but I\'m considering what happens if the environment variable is actually undefined, or if Vite fails to perform the replacement.\n\n\n**Investigating `@google/genai` Usage**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the `@google/genai` package itself, suspecting direct Node `process` usage within its source code. I\'m trying to determine whether the package is directly calling Node\'s `process` within its source code. My focus is on understanding this dependency and how the library handles environment variables, specifically in a browser context, which appears to be causing issues. I am also looking at how we can handle this properly given our architecture.\n\n\n**Implementing Server-Side Route**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a promising direction! The plan is to create a server-side route, `/api/gemini`, within an Express server, to call the Gemini API. This approach allows us to keep `GEMINI_API_KEY` secure on the server and move the `@google/genai` dependency there as well. The frontend then calls the backend API to access Gemini which completely eliminates the browser-side crash and keeps us secure! I\'m verifying `@google/genai` is the source of the crash.\n\n\n**Exploring Full-Stack Possibilities**\n\nI\'m now considering a full-stack approach. The absence of a `server.ts` file initially raised concerns, but the framework guidelines propose a path to migrate to a Vite + Express setup, which looks like the direction to pursue. I\'m checking the `package.json` file for the necessary dependencies: I see `tsx` and `express` are in dependencies, but `esbuild` is not. This will have to be added to the dependencies for the implementation of the server.\n\n\n**Implementing Server-Side Route**\n\nI\'m solidifying the plan: I must rewrite `aiService.ts` to call a local API route, specifically `/api/gemini`. I\'ll create a new `server.ts` file to host a server-side endpoint `/api/gemini/insights`. This approach allows the frontend to call this new backend API, effectively relocating `@google/genai` usage and addressing the browser-side crash.\n\n\n**Refining Client-Side Function**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the client-side `aiService.ts` function. I\'ve re-worked it to use `fetch` to call a new `/api/gemini/insights` endpoint on the server. This completely removes the direct import of `@google/genai` from the browser-side code, which eliminates the runtime crashes. The function now sends a POST request with the context information and handles the response, including error handling.\n\n\n**Constructing Server Implementation**\n\nI\'m now writing the `server.ts` file, a standard Express server with Vite middleware. It will include a `/api/gemini/insights` route to handle the Gemini API call securely. I have added `dotenv` to load the `GEMINI_API_KEY` and am integrating `@google/genai`. I\'m using an error handling response that sends a 400 error if `contextInfo` is missing, and a 500 error if the API key is not configured on the server.\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining the server-side error handling for the `/api/gemini/insights` route.  I\'ve added specific error messages and logging to the `catch` block for clearer debugging.  I\'m also ensuring the Gemini API call includes the context information. The endpoint now provides actionable insights in markdown format, as intended.\n\n\n"
