  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Potential Issues**\n\nI\'m thrilled with the initial progress, however, I\'m now digging into potential edge cases. Specifically, I\'m simulating scenarios. What happens if the user loads the app right now? How does this impact the loading process, starting with `index.html`, and how the browser subsequently requests the `/src/main` directory? I want to ensure this behaves as expected.\n\n\n**Verifying Runtime Environment**\n\nI\'ve made significant strides in eliminating client-side Node.js environment issues. I\'ve confirmed that `main.tsx` no longer directly or indirectly imports `@google/genai`, and that the API key is secured on the server. The loader screen gives way to React seamlessly. I am currently double-checking the codebase for any remaining client-side references to `aiService` or any use of `process.env`. Specifically, I searched the codebase for \"process.env\" again to ensure I didn\'t miss anything, and it turns out, there are NO references. I also re-examined `index.html` and `vite.config.ts` for safety.\n\n\n**Confirming Perfect Operation**\n\nI\'m checking that the development server runs without any issues, especially concerning the Cesium plugin. Everything appears to be working flawlessly after the restart. It seems the complete problem has been solved and all system guidelines were followed, which I\'ve now documented, and I\'m ready to continue writing!\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I have successfully diagnosed and resolved the runtime loading issue in the TeraFlowSDN Control Plane application.\n\n### Root Cause Analysis\nThe application was configured as a client-only Single Page Application (SPA), but its frontend statically imported the `@google/genai` SDK in browser-facing code (`aiService.ts`). Because the `@google/genai` library relies on server-side Node.js globals and network environments, bundling it for the browser caused a silent runtime mounting exception in the browser\'s module evaluation phase. This prevented `/src/main.tsx` from initiating or rendering any React components, keeping the interface indefinitely stuck on the static boot screen.\n\n### Implemented Solutions\n\n1. **Transitioned to a Full-Stack Architecture**:\n   - Outlined and implemented a native, standard full-stack configuration combining **Vite** with a secure **Express** server (`server.ts`).\n   - Standardized development, compilation, and standalone launch controls within `package.json` utilizing `tsx` and `esbuild`.\n\n2. **Created Secure Server-Side Proxy Endpoint**:\n   - Introduced a new backend API endpoint `/api/gemini/insights` in `server.ts` to handle and process natural language insights.\n   - Initialized and executed the `@google/genai` pipeline safely on the server side, keeping your **Gemini API Key** completely hidden from the browser.\n\n3. **Refactored Client-Side Service**:\n   - Standardized `generateAIInsights` inside `src/services/aiService.ts` to fetch directly from the local `/api/gemini/insights` endpoint.\n   - Stripped away all client-side dependencies on `@google/genai`, solving the browser mounting/compilation blocks.\n\n4. **Verification & Activation**:\n   - ran the TypeScript diagnostic linter (`lint_applet`) and verified the entire full-stack build pipeline completes successfully (`compile_applet`).\n   - Restarted the Node.js development server to pick up the active Express proxy on port `3000`."
