  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Client-Side Distribution**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the implications of a fully client-side application. The core question is distribution and installation \342\200\223 how to get this built application running on other systems. I need to consider options for distribution methods, without server-side components.\n\n\n**Defining Distribution Model**\n\nI\'ve determined the distribution model for this client-side application. The compiled static files, created by the build step, reside in the `dist` directory. This is easily hosted on static web servers, such as GitHub Pages or Netlify. This also includes extremely cheap solutions, since there\'s no server-side component.\n\n\n**Exploring Deployment Options**\n\nI\'m now exploring specific deployment options for my client-side application. I see several possibilities: deploying directly from a Git repository to platforms like Vercel, Netlify, or Cloudflare Pages. Alternatively, I could use Google Cloud Storage or AWS S3 buckets with website hosting. For local installations, a lightweight Nginx container looks viable. And finally, considering PWA or Electron/Capacitor shells for desktop/mobile applications.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "An application that is compiled as a **fully client-side Single-Page Application (SPA)** contains no runtime container or Node.js server dependencies. Instead, the build step (`npm run build`) compiles all TypeScript, JSX, global CSS, and asset imports (such as Cesium or map engines) into standard, highly optimized static files (HTML, JS, CSS) inside a single `/dist` folder.\n\nThis structure makes distribution, hosting, and installation exceptionally flexible and cost-effective across environments:\n\n### 1. Simple Web Distribution (Static Hosting)\nBecause the application is static, you do not need an application server to serve or run it. You can distribute it by simply deploying the `/dist` directory to:\n*   **Static CDNs & Platforms**: Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, or GitHub Pages.\n*   **Object Storage**: Google Cloud Storage (GCS) buckets, AWS S3 with Website Hosting enabled, or Azure Blob Storage. This represents an ultra-low-cost, infinitely scalable deployment model.\n\n### 2. Enterprise / On-Premise Installation\nTo host the app locally on a private network, virtual machine, or local server, the most common standard is to bundle the static files with a tiny web server like Nginx inside a lightweight Docker container:\n```dockerfile\n# Simple Dockerfile to distribute the application\nFROM nginx:alpine\nCOPY dist /usr/share/nginx/html\nEXPOSE 80\nCMD [\"nginx\", \"-g\", \"daemon off;\"]\n```\nThis container compiles down to just a few megabytes, starts instantly, and can be distributed as a standard image to run anywhere.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation Plan for Restoring a Fully Client-Side Architecture\n\nTo revert the current setup back to a standard, pristine client-side-only architecture and run Vite directly in the browser, here is the step-by-step implementation plan for your approval:\n\n*   **Step 1: Revert `package.json` Configuration**  \n    Restore the original scripts to use Vite\'s runtime engine directly without any server-side compilation wrapper:\n    *   `\"dev\"`: `vite --port=3000 --host=0.0.0.0 --force`\n    *   `\"build\"`: `vite build`\n    *   Remove custom CJS outputs, `esbuild` dependencies, and custom starts.\n\n*   **Step 2: Remove Backend Server Code**  \n    *   Delete the `/server.ts` file completely from the workspace.\n\n*   **Step 3: Client-Side Stubbing for AI Components**  \n    *   Since Gemini is not in use, simplify `src/services/aiService.ts` to return static mock network telemetry suggestions or return a standard message (e.g. *\"AI Insights disabled: App is running in client-only mode\"*).\n    *   Ensure that clicking any AI-related cards on the Dashboard or Detail panels safely displays a clean fallback message without attempting server HTTP calls or raising CORS/network errors.\n\n***\n\n**Would you like me to proceed with executing this client-side clean-up plan?**"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 3
      nanos: 601883751
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
