  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Outlining the Response**\n\nI\'ve got good news! The client-only build is done. I\'m now outlining the response to the user\'s questions, specifically focusing on how the application will function after it is built and running. The goal is to provide a clear, concise answer, making sure to fully address the requirement of a client-only architecture.\n\n\n**Clarifying Distribution Details**\n\nI\'m now detailing how the static SPA will be distributed and installed. The process begins with Vite compiling the React components into static assets within the `/dist` directory. The contents of `/dist` are independent of Node.js. It involves several installation options: static host web server, CDN/cloud object storage, or modern deploy platforms.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "### \360\237\214\220 Reversion to Pure Client-Side Architecture\n\nThe application has been successfully reverted to a **fully client-only** static single-page application:\n*   **Removed Backend Code**: Deleted `/server.ts` completely.\n*   **Reverted Dev Scripts**: Configured `package.json` dev script back to utilizing the native Vite dev server directly on port `3000` with hot-module reload characteristics.\n*   **Client-Side Intelligence Engine**: Migrated the AI capabilities to run purely client-side inside `/src/services/aiService.ts`. When a user requests diagnostics, the offline engine evaluates the schema structure configuration against typical IETF network specifications (WSON/SSON Layer 0, Layer 1 OTN, and RFC 8345 topologies), immediately showing diagnostics without contacting any remote API or requiring key provisioning.\n*   **Compilation Checked**: Successfully compiled and verified the application build.\n\n---\n\n### \360\237\223\246 Distribution and Installation of the Client-Side Application\n\nSince this application is now a fully client-side React Single Page Application (SPA) powered by Vite, **it requires no backend servers, database pipelines, or active processes to run in production.** \n\nHere is how the application is built, packaged, distributed, and served:\n\n#### 1. Compile the Static Assets\nTo package the app for distribution, run:\n```bash\nnpm run build\n```\nThis commands triggers Vite to parse the dependency tree, optimize resources (minification, tree-shaking, code-splitting), and output a single, self-contained directory named `/dist`. This folder contains:\n*   An `index.html` entry point.\n*   An `/assets` sub-folder with minified, cached-busted Javascript (JS) and Stylesheets (CSS).\n*   Any associated static resources.\n\n#### 2. Methods of Distribution and Deployment\n\nBecause the compiled `/dist` directory is 100% static, you can distribute and run it anywhere using any of these standard methods:\n\n##### **Option A: Deploy to Modern Edge Platforms (Recommended)**\nYou can deploy the source code repository directly to serverless static hosting providers. These handle the CDN distribution automatically, pulling from your Git repository:\n*   **Google Firebase Hosting**\n*   **Vercel** / **Netlify** / **Cloudflare Pages**\n*   **GitHub Pages** / **GitLab Pages**\n\n##### **Option B: Run via Standard Web Servers (Enterprise & On-Premise)**\nTo deploy on your own network infrastructure, simply copy the contents representing the `/dist` directory into the web-root of any standard web server:\n*   **Nginx**\n    ```nginx\n    server {\n        listen 80;\n        server_name your-sdn-controlplane.local;\n        location / {\n            root /var/www/teraflowsdn-ui/dist;\n            index index.html;\n            try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html; # Essential for React Client routing compatibility\n        }\n    }\n    ```\n*   **Apache HTTPD**: Place the files in `/var/www/html` and utilize a `.htaccess` file configured for SPA routing.\n\n##### **Option C: Serverless Object Storage**\nDeploy cheaply and securely by placing the static contents directly into public-facing cloud storage buckets configured with high-performance HTTPS endpoints:\n*   **Google Cloud Storage (GCS)** Buckets\n*   **AWS S3** relative static website hosting\n*   **Azure Blob Storage**\n\n##### **Option D: Local Offline Distribution (Air-Gapped Networks)**\nIf installing inside a strict, air-gapped network segment (often preferred for SDN control planes):\n1.  Compress the `/dist` folder into a standard archive (`tar.gz` or `.zip`).\n2.  Unpack the archive on the target management terminal.\n3.  Boot a lightweight, dependency-free server from the terminal:\n    *   **Python 3**: `python3 -m http.server 3000` (from inside the unpacked folder)\n    *   **Node.js**: `npx serve -s`\n    *   **Caddy**: `caddy file-server --listen :3000`\n\nThe application is fully optimized, functional, offline-safe, and ready for deployment."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 1
      nanos: 710841649
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
