  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Clarifying Distribution Methods**\n\nI\'ve got the `index.css` configuration nailed down. Now, I\'m focusing on the prompt \342\200\223 specifically, how to distribute and install a fully client-side application. I need to make sure this step is super clear, so I\'m outlining that portion more precisely!\n\n\n**Interpreting Build Feedback**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on interpreting the build feedback within the prompt. The user\'s prompt mentions a build failure due to rate limits. However, the last two operations reported success. It\'s likely the platform is providing automated error messages that aren\'t representative of the last operations.\n\n\n**Analyzing Authentication Flow**\n\nOkay, I\'m now dissecting the authentication flow to pinpoint potential user experience bottlenecks. I\'ve drilled into the `AuthContext.tsx` file and outlined the login process. The initial null state of the user, the 500ms delay, and the reliance on `localStorage` for `mockUser` are key points to assess. It\'s likely that a user would be redirected if their `mockUser` is not present in local storage.\n\n\n**Confirming Client-Side Operation**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the application\'s entirely client-side nature, as requested. The \"Continue with Google\" button triggers `signInWithGoogle()`, which simulates user authentication using `localStorage` for session persistence. After a 600ms loading period, a mock user is created, stored, and the user gains access to the dashboard. The application is now fully functional, meeting the specified requirements!\n\n\n**Addressing Build Failures**\n\nI\'m now formulating a concise, helpful response. I will clearly explain that the `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED` error indicates a transient Google Cloud Run rate limit from rapid builds. I\'ll confirm clean compilation and linting, then address the distribution and installation question directly. I plan to detail how a client-side React + Vite SPA is distributed and hosted, including options like GitHub Pages, Vercel, Netlify, Cloud Run, or local opening, and the export process from AI Studio.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "The build error you witnessed in the deployment logs\342\200\224**`RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED: Rate exceeded`**\342\200\224is a transient, platform-level rate-limit warning. It occurs within the cloud control plane when multiple dev server restarts or build commands are fired in rapid succession.\n\nThere are **no errors inside your application code**. I have executed both strict TypeScript validation and complete application builds to verify the integrity:\n*   **Verification Success**: The code compiled successfully with **0 errors**.\n*   **Conformance Validation**: The linter executed cleanly with **0 issues**.\n\nSince this is a fully client-side single-page application (SPA), here is how it is built, distributed, and installed elsewhere.\n\n---\n\n### SPA Distribution and Installation Plan\n\nWhen your client-side application is built, Vite compiles all TypeScript, React, and Tailwind CSS files into a **static, optimized web bundle**. \n\n#### 1. Build Output\nRunning `npm run build` generates a single directory named **`dist/`** which contains:\n*   An optimized `index.html` file.\n*   Minified JavaScript files containing your React components and the client-side diagnostic mock engine.\n*   A consolidated, purged Tailwind CSS bundle.\n\n#### 2. Exporting from Google AI Studio\nYou can download your clean client-only workspace at any time to run it locally or migrate it to another system:\n*   Open the **Settings Menu** (represented by the cog icon in the top right of the editor).\n*   Select **Download ZIP** to receive the complete project files, or **Export to GitHub** to push the codebase directly to a remote repository.\n\n#### 3. Distribution and Hosting Options\nBecause the app produces purely static assets, you do not need backend hosting or containers. You can run and distribute it anywhere using these methods:\n\n*   **Static Web Hosting (Zero Cost)**: Simply drag and drop the `dist/` folder onto hosting services like **Vercel**, **Netlify**, **Cloudflare Pages**, or **GitHub Pages**. They will serve the files directly from a global Content Delivery Network (CDN).\n*   **Enterprise Intranet / Local Server**: Any standard web server capable of serving static assets (such as **Nginx**, **Apache**, or a lightweight Node.js/Python server) can serve this application. The files can even be launched directly from the local file system.\n*   **Client-Side Persistence**: User session data, local inventory, and schema validation statuses survive page reloads naturally on the client, as the application utilizes standard browser **`localStorage`** APIs."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 793922796
    }
