  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Server Response**\n\nOkay, I\'m trying to figure out these 429 errors. I\'ve encountered a WebSocket handshake failure, specifically mentioning the development server in a container behind our reverse proxy. It seems like the core issue might be related to WebSocket setup within the reverse proxy configuration. Let\'s delve deeper into that.\n\n\n**Investigating the Rate Limiting**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a possible rate-limiting issue. The Vite\'s HMR WebSocket might be spamming connection attempts, causing the reverse proxy to block further requests from that IP. I believe the rapid loop of failed WebSocket attempts is triggering a 429 error on the platform proxy. The error messages, \"Sec-WebSocket-Accept\' header is missing\" and \"Unexpected response code: 429\", support this theory. The user\'s browser, relentlessly attempting WebSocket connections that fail, then triggers the rate limit.\n\n\n**Unpacking the Root Cause**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the cross-cutting causes. The 429 errors aren\'t isolated to WebSocket handshakes; regular asset fetches like JS and CSS are also getting blocked. That\'s odd. The core is the `DISABLE_HMR` environment variable, which influences whether Vite\'s HMR WebSocket is active or not. If HMR is disabled, why is the browser still trying to connect to WSS? I\'ve confirmed Vite is configured to disable HMR via the `DISABLE_HMR` env var, but the crucial question is whether this environment variable is correctly set during the build. If `hmr` ends up as `true`, `@vite/client` gets injected, and the connection attempts ensue, even when we don\'t want them.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the WebSocket Problem**\n\nOkay, I\'m now honing in on the WebSocket issue. My thinking now is that the reverse proxy might not be configured to properly handle WebSockets on the root path, leading to missing headers and `@vite/client`\'s retries. It seems I am on the right track! The catch-and-retry logic is an important factor. To halt the WebSocket loop, I\'m going to set `hmr: false` directly in `vite.config.ts`, or handle it dynamically. I\'ll test if this stops the WebSocket attempts, eliminating the 429 errors. I am also looking at the repository to see how HMR is handled in `vite.config.ts`.\n\n\n**Confirming the Configuration**\n\nI am now focusing on confirming the configuration. I\'m investigating why HMR might still be active despite `DISABLE_HMR=true`. I suspect the environment variable isn\'t propagating correctly to the dev server process, or there\'s an issue with the string representation or undefined values. Alternatively, `@vite/client` might be running regardless. To ensure HMR is disabled, I\'ll hardcode `hmr: false` in `vite.config.ts`, regardless of the environment variable\'s value. That should fully eliminate the WebSocket attempts and, hopefully, the 429 errors.\n\n\n**Identifying the Platform WebSocket**\n\nI\'ve just uncovered something interesting. I thought the problem was Vite\'s HMR WebSocket. However, I\'ve realized the failing WebSocket is within a platform-injected script, `_aistudio-iframe.js`. It looks like this script handles communication between the iframe container and the platform, for logs and sync. The \"Sec-WebSocket-Accept\" missing header still points to a reverse proxy configuration problem.\n\n\n**Connecting the Error Chain**\n\nOkay, so I\'m now understanding how everything connects! If the platform\'s `_aistudio-iframe.js` experiences a WebSocket handshake failure and triggers the rate limit (429), it means the server is applying the 429 status to every subsequent request, not just the iframe script! And I see now! The problem is that the Vite HMR was also running, leading to 200+ requests.\n\n\n**Unveiling the Underlying Cause**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial breakthrough! I\'ve realized Vite\'s dev mode, with its individual file requests (hundreds for a large app), can quickly trigger our routing proxy\'s rate limit. With hundreds of requests from a single browser simultaneously, we\'re essentially DDoS-ing ourselves in development. This explains the 429 errors! It\'s a fundamental issue of how Vite dev mode works in our setup.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Route to Solution**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the implications of serving a bundled production version during development to bypass the rate limits. I\'ve realized a standard `npm run dev` in development mode generates hundreds of requests due to individual file imports, but using a `vite build` bundles the app into a small number of files. Serving the production build drastically reduces the request count. I\'m also now considering the platform\'s restrictions, particularly the port 3000 limitation. I\'m investigating how this limitation interacts with serving a production build in the development environment.\n\n\n**Implementing the Build Solution**\n\nI\'m now implementing the solution. If the PORT value, 3000, is hardcoded, I should run a simple, lightweight server to serve compiled static files from `dist/` on port 3000. Our repository doesn\'t have a `server.ts` file, and I\'ll check `package.json` to understand the setup. I am thinking this is a pure React SPA.\n\n\n**Refining the Dev Configuration**\n\nI\'m now refining the development configuration. It turns out that, since we use an SPA setup, we do not have to provide a \"start\" script in `package.json`. In the repository, the instructions state that a static file server is automatically injected for SPAs. The `\"dev\"` script is the problem! It\'s running the Vite dev server, which triggers the rate limit with its individual file requests. The instructions also provide an `restart_dev_server` command after making changes to the \"dev\" script. My plan is to modify it to use the production build.\n\n\n**Testing the Solution**\n\nI\'m now putting the solution to the test. I\'ve modified the `dev` script to first build the app using `vite build` and then serve it using `vite preview` on port 3000.  The crucial part is the `npm run build && vite preview --port=3000 --host=0.0.0.0` part.  This way, the browser should now only load a few bundled files, rather than all those individual `.tsx` files. I am confident that this resolves the 429 rate limiting.  I\'m expecting to see a huge reduction in requests when I reload the dev environment. I am thinking, I might be right.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view `/src/services/networkService.ts` to see if there are any network requests or API polling loops that might cause a flood of requests."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 17
      nanos: 911079596
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
