      thinking_budget: 4000
    }
    started_at {
      seconds: 1780811854
      nanos: 410355722
    }
  }
  planner_response {
    modified_response: "### What Happened and Why the Preview Stopped Working\n\nEarlier, your preview was running Vite in a live on-the-fly development mode. This is fine for small apps, but as our application grew and integrated substantial libraries (such as Cesium, Resium, Leaflet, D3, Recharts, etc.), the number of separate source files and assets dramatically increased. \n\nThis led to two major network issues:\n1. **HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests)**: When loading the app, Vite dev mode requested 100+ separate module files in a fraction of a second. The Google Cloud Run reverse proxy/gateway intercepted this sudden surge of requests as a potential flood and automatically triggered rate-limiting, blocking further assets from loading. This caused a blank screen or broken layout.\n2. **WebSocket Handshake Failures**: Vite\'s built-in developer HMR (Hot Module Replacement) attempted to establish a persistent WebSocket connection (`wss://...`). Because the preview environment runs behind a highly secured reverse proxy, these WebSocket handshakes were blocked, triggering rapid connection retries that further consumed connection limits and triggered rate limits.\n\n---\n\n### How I Fixed It\n\nI modified the development configuration in **`package.json`** and **`vite.config.ts`** to bypass these platform limitations completely:\n\n- **Optimized Bundle and Preview**: I updated the `\"dev\"` script inside your `package.json` to:\n  ```json\n  \"dev\": \"vite build && vite preview --port=3000 --host=0.0.0.0\"\n  ```\n  Now, when the container boots or restarts, Vite pre-bundles the entire application into highly optimized single static files in the `/dist` directory. \n- **Eliminated the WebSocket Loop**: By serving the app under `vite preview`, no HMR WebSocket scripts are injected into the client browser, completely eliminating the WebSocket connection errors and unnecessary rate-limiting triggers.\n- **Single-Request Load**: Now, when you open the preview, your browser downloads a single optimized static bundle instead of 100+ individual files. This prevents the `429` rate-splitting limits entirely and ensures that your application loads flawlessly.\n\n---\n\n### Current Status\n\n- **Compilation**: Successfully compiled and build checks passed with zero errors (`tsc --noEmit` and Vite bundle built dynamically).\n- **Service Status**: The development server was restarted and is running the optimized build mode.\n- **Application Live URL**: The application is active and running on your dev server URL at **port 3000**.\n\nPlease refresh your browser page or preview frame to see the fully restored, fast-loading, rate-limit-free version of your application! We can now continue to code and build out features seamlessly."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
